<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
║  GetBook.wsdl  — CONTRACT FOR THE GetBook OPERATION                          ║
╠══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╣
║                                                                              ║
║  WHAT IS A WSDL FILE?                                                        ║
║  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── ║
║  WSDL = Web Services Description Language                                    ║
║                                                                              ║
║  A WSDL file is the CONTRACT of a SOAP service — a formal machine-readable   ║
║  document that answers the question: "How do I talk to this service?"        ║
║                                                                              ║
║  It describes:                                                               ║
║    • WHAT operations exist          → GetBook                                ║
║    • WHAT data to send              → an ISBN string                         ║
║    • WHAT data you get back         → a full Book object                     ║
║    • WHAT happens when it goes wrong→ a WsFault with errorCode/message       ║
║    • HOW to encode the messages     → SOAP / document / literal              ║
║    • WHERE the service lives        → http://book-server:8080/getbook        ║
║                                                                              ║
║  A WSDL has 5 sections in order:                                             ║
║    1. <wsdl:types>    — imports external XSD files (the data shapes)         ║
║    2. <wsdl:message>  — named groups of data parts                           ║
║    3. <wsdl:portType> — abstract list of operations (the interface)          ║
║    4. <wsdl:binding>  — how portType maps to SOAP on the wire                ║
║    5. <wsdl:service>  — the actual network address (URL)                     ║
║                                                                              ║
║  The sections chain together like this:                                      ║
║    <types>    defines element shapes                                         ║
║        ↑ referenced by                                                       ║
║    <message>  gives names to data packages                                   ║
║        ↑ referenced by                                                       ║
║    <portType> lists operations and their input/output/fault messages         ║
║        ↑ referenced by                                                       ║
║    <binding>  maps portType to SOAP/HTTP                                     ║
║        ↑ referenced by                                                       ║
║    <service>  provides the concrete URL                                      ║
║                                                                              ║
║  IMPORTANT — TWO WSDL USAGES IN THIS PROJECT:                                ║
║    1. BUILD TIME: wsimport reads the local copy of this file                 ║
║       (book-client/src/main/resources/wsdl/GetBook.wsdl)                     ║
║       to generate Java client stub classes. The server does NOT need to      ║
║       be running for this step.                                              ║
║    2. RUNTIME: the generated client connects to the live server WSDL at      ║
║       http://book-server:8080/getbook?wsdl to resolve the endpoint URL.      ║
║       The server MUST be running for this step.                              ║
║                                                                              ║
║  XSD FILES IMPORTED BY THIS WSDL:                                            ║
║    • book.xsd    → GetBookInput, GetBookOutput (and all other book ops)      ║
║    • common.xsd  → WsFault, WsCredentials.java                               ║
║                                                                              ║
║  NAMESPACE PREFIXES USED IN THIS FILE:                                       ║
║    wsdl: → standard WSDL elements  (wsdl:types, wsdl:message, etc.)          ║
║    soap: → SOAP binding elements   (soap:binding, soap:body, etc.)           ║
║    tns:  → this namespace          (our messages, portType, binding)         ║
║    bk:   → book XSD namespace      (GetBookInput, GetBookOutput, etc.)       ║
║    cmn:  → common XSD namespace    (WsFault, WsCredentials.java)             ║
╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
-->

<!--
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
<wsdl:definitions> — THE ROOT ELEMENT
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
Everything in a WSDL is wrapped inside <wsdl:definitions>.

ATTRIBUTES:

xmlns:wsdl="http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/wsdl/"
  → Declares the "wsdl:" prefix for standard WSDL structural elements.
    Elements like <wsdl:types>, <wsdl:message>, <wsdl:portType>, <wsdl:binding>,
    and <wsdl:service> all belong to this namespace.
    This URL IS a real specification document (you can visit it in a browser).

    NOTE: In simpler WSDLs you may see xmlns="http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/wsdl/"
    (no prefix) which makes WSDL elements unqualified: <types>, <message>, etc.
    Here we use the explicit wsdl: prefix — more verbose but clearer in large
    files where XSD, SOAP, and WSDL elements are all mixed together.
    Enterprise SOAP tools (IBM WebSphere, SAP, Oracle) use this convention.

xmlns:soap="http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/wsdl/soap/"
  → Declares the "soap:" prefix for SOAP-specific binding elements.
    Elements like <soap:binding>, <soap:body>, <soap:address>, <soap:fault>
    come from this namespace. When you see "soap:" it is a SOAP-specific
    instruction, not a generic WSDL structural element.

xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
  → The XML Schema namespace. Used to reference built-in XSD types and
    to write <xsd:import> and <xsd:schema> elements inside <wsdl:types>.
    Examples of built-in types: xsd:string, xsd:int, xsd:decimal, xsd:dateTime.
    http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema IS a real URL (W3C specification).

xmlns:tns="http://bookservice.com/getbook/wsdl/v1"
  → "tns" = "this namespace" — a conventional abbreviation.
    This is THIS WSDL's own namespace — the one declared by targetNamespace.
    We use "tns:" to reference things defined IN THIS FILE:
      tns:GetBookRequest  → the <wsdl:message> we define below
      tns:GetBookPortType → the <wsdl:portType> we define below
      tns:GetBookBinding  → the <wsdl:binding> we define below
    REMEMBER: http://bookservice.com/getbook/wsdl/v1 is NOT a real URL to visit.
    It is just a unique identifier string that looks like a URL.
    By convention, it looks like a URL to guarantee global uniqueness.

xmlns:bk="http://bookservice.com/book/v1"
  → Prefix for the book XSD namespace (defined in book.xsd).
    Used to reference elements imported from book.xsd:
      bk:GetBookInput  → the request data shape  (isbn field)
      bk:GetBookOutput → the response data shape (book field)
    "bk:" is just an alias — you could call it anything, but short and
    meaningful prefixes make the file readable.

xmlns:cmn="http://bookservice.com/common/v1"
  → Prefix for the common XSD namespace (defined in common.xsd).
    Used to reference shared elements:
      cmn:WsFault       → the fault detail element (errorCode, errorMessage, etc.)
      cmn:WsCredentials.java → the authentication header element

name="GetBookService"
  → A human-readable name for this WSDL document. Informational only.
    Has no impact on generated code or runtime behaviour.

targetNamespace="http://bookservice.com/getbook/wsdl/v1"
  → Declares that all elements DEFINED IN THIS FILE belong to this namespace.
    This is what "tns:" points to. All our <wsdl:message>, <wsdl:portType>,
    <wsdl:binding>, and <wsdl:service> definitions are "owned" by this namespace.
    The WSDL namespace is SEPARATE from the XSD namespaces (bk:, cmn:) —
    each layer of the stack has its own namespace:
      WSDL layer  → http://bookservice.com/getbook/wsdl/v1    (this file)
      Book XSD    → http://bookservice.com/book/v1            (book.xsd)
      Common XSD  → http://bookservice.com/common/v1          (common.xsd)
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-->
<wsdl:definitions
        xmlns:wsdl="http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/wsdl/"
        xmlns:soap="http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/wsdl/soap/"
        xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
        xmlns:tns="http://bookservice.com/getbook/wsdl/v1"
        xmlns:bk="http://bookservice.com/book/v1"
        xmlns:cmn="http://bookservice.com/common/v1"
        name="GetBookService"
        targetNamespace="http://bookservice.com/getbook/wsdl/v1">


    <!--
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    SECTION 1: <wsdl:types>
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    This section provides the XSD type definitions for all messages.
    Instead of defining types INLINE here (which is valid but messy), we
    IMPORT external XSD files. This keeps the WSDL clean and allows the same
    XSD to be reused by multiple WSDLs (e.g. CreateBook.wsdl also imports
    book.xsd and common.xsd).

    WHY do we need a <xsd:schema> wrapper around the imports?
      <xsd:import> is an XSD element. XSD elements must appear inside an
      <xsd:schema> context — even when that schema itself defines no types
      of its own and only exists to hold import statements.
      The wrapper <xsd:schema> here has NO targetNamespace of its own.
      It is just a "shell" or "import container".

    HOW does schemaLocation work?
      schemaLocation is a HINT to the XML parser about where to find the file.
      It is a relative path from the location of THIS wsdl file.
      If GetBook.wsdl and book.xsd sit in the same directory, just the filename
      is enough. In other projects it might be "../xsd/book.xsd".
      Some parsers can resolve schemas by namespace alone (using an XML
      catalog) — in that case schemaLocation can be omitted, but we always
      include it for clarity and portability.
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    -->
    <wsdl:types>
        <xsd:schema>

            <!--
            Import 1: book.xsd — all book operation types.
            Brings into scope: GetBookInput, GetBookOutput,
            CreateBookInput, CreateBookOutput (all in one shared file).

            namespace must match targetNamespace in book.xsd exactly:
              "http://bookservice.com/book/v1"
            After this import, "bk:" prefix resolves to that namespace, and
            we can write bk:GetBookInput in the <wsdl:message> section below.
            -->
            <xsd:import
                    namespace="http://bookservice.com/book/v1"
                    schemaLocation="book.xsd"/>

            <!--
            Import 2: common.xsd — shared types used by all operations.
            Brings into scope: WsFault, WsCredentials.java.

            What happens if book.xsd already imports internally common.xsd?
            Should I import it here again?
            Rule: if THIS file references a namespace, it must import it.
            This WSDL directly references cmn:WsFault in the WsException
            message below. So we must import common.xsd here regardless of
            what book.xsd does internally.
            -->
            <xsd:import
                    namespace="http://bookservice.com/common/v1"
                    schemaLocation="common.xsd"/>
        </xsd:schema>
    </wsdl:types>


    <!--
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    SECTION 2: <wsdl:message>
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    A <wsdl:message> is a NAMED WRAPPER around one or more data parts.
    It connects a meaningful name (used by portType operations) to the actual
    XSD element that defines the data shape.

    Think of <wsdl:message> as: "I am giving a name to this data package
    so the portType can refer to it by name."

    In document/literal style there is always ONE <wsdl:part> per message.
    That part references one top-level element from the imported XSD files.

    STRUCTURE:
      <wsdl:message name="...">           ← WSDL-level name
        <wsdl:part element="ns:..."       ← XSD-level element (the data shape)
                   name="..."/>           ← internal label for this part
      </wsdl:message>

    NAMING CLARITY — two distinct levels:
      Message name   "GetBookRequest"  → the WSDL-level concept (a request)
      Part element   "bk:GetBookInput" → the XSD-level element (the data shape)
      Part name      "GetBookInput"    → internal label matching the element name
                                         Usually it look like this: name="elements"

    WHY keep "Request/Response" for the message and "Input/Output" for the part?
      The message is the ENVELOPE CONCEPT  → "this is a request message"
      The element is the DATA CONCEPT      → "this is the input data shape"
      Keeping these names distinct prevents confusion in large projects with
      many operations where names would otherwise collide.

    We have THREE messages for this one operation:
      GetBookRequest  → what the client sends to the server
      GetBookResponse → what the server sends back to the client
      WsException     → what the server sends when something goes wrong
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    -->

    <!-- The request message: client → server ─────────────────────────────── -->
    <wsdl:message name="GetBookRequest">
        <!--
       element="bk:GetBookInput"
         → "bk:" resolves to "http://bookservice.com/book/v1"
         → References <xsd:element name="GetBookInput"> from book.xsd
         → That element defines the INPUT data shape: contains one <isbn> field

         name="GetBookInput"
         → Internal label for this part. By convention, it mirrors the element
           name. In document/literal style this label is not visible in the
           SOAP XML — it is only used internally within the WSDL.
       -->
        <wsdl:part element="bk:GetBookInput" name="GetBookInput"/>
    </wsdl:message>

    <!-- The response message: server → client -->
    <wsdl:message name="GetBookResponse">
        <!--
        element="bk:GetBookOutput"
          → References <xsd:element name="GetBookOutput"> from book.xsd
          → That element defines the OUTPUT data shape: contains a <book> field
            of type bk:BookType (isbn, title, author, price, stockQuantity, genre)
        -->
        <wsdl:part element="bk:GetBookOutput" name="GetBookOutput"/>
    </wsdl:message>

    <!-- The fault message: server → client (when an error occurs) ─────────── -->
    <!--
    WsException is a SHARED fault message used by ALL operations in this service.
    Both GetBook and CreateBook declare this same fault.
    The element="cmn:WsFault" references the fault structure defined in common.xsd:
      <cmn:errorCode>      → machine-readable code  e.g. "BOOK_NOT_FOUND"
      <cmn:errorMessage>   → human-readable message
      <cmn:errorTimestamp> → when the error occurred
      <cmn:operationName>  → which operation threw the fault

    On the wire this fault becomes:
      <soapenv:Fault>
        <faultcode>soapenv:Server</faultcode>
        <faultstring>No book found with ISBN: 978-x</faultstring>
        <detail>
          <cmn:WsFault>
            <cmn:errorCode>BOOK_NOT_FOUND</cmn:errorCode>
            ...
          </cmn:WsFault>
        </detail>
      </soapenv:Fault>
    -->
    <wsdl:message name="WsException">
        <wsdl:part name="WsFault" element="cmn:WsFault"/>
    </wsdl:message>


    <!--
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    SECTION 3: <wsdl:portType>
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    The portType is the ABSTRACT INTERFACE of the service.
    It lists the available operations and for each one: what goes in,
    what comes out, and what can go wrong.
    It says WHAT operations exist — NOT how they are transmitted over the wire.
    The "how" is handled by <wsdl:binding> in Section 4.

    This is the WSDL equivalent of a Java interface.
    name="GetBookPortType"
      → Maps to the Java SEI interface: GetBookPortType.java
        The "PortType" suffix is a real-world enterprise convention that makes
        it immediately clear: "this is a WSDL portType mapping, not a service
        class or a utility."

    Each <wsdl:operation> inside portType:
      → Represents one callable SOAP operation (one Java method on the SEI)
      → Has an <wsdl:input>  referencing the request message
      → Has an <wsdl:output> referencing the response message
      → Has an <wsdl:fault>  referencing the error message

    This is how the sections chain together:
      portType operation
        → references message (by tns: name)
          → message references XSD element (by bk: or cmn: name)
            → XSD element defines the actual data shape
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    -->
    <wsdl:portType name="GetBookPortType">
        <wsdl:operation name="GetBook">

            <!--
            <wsdl:input>
              name="GetBookRequest"
                → Labels this input within the operation.
                  By convention, it matches the message name.
                  message="tns:GetBookRequest"
                → References the <wsdl:message name="GetBookRequest"> above.
                  "tns:" = "this namespace" = http://bookservice.com/getbook/wsdl/v1
                  This is how the portType and message sections are linked.
            -->
            <wsdl:input  message="tns:GetBookRequest"  name="GetBookRequest"/>

            <!--
           <wsdl:output>
             When the operation succeeds, the server sends GetBookResponse.
             This contains the GetBookOutput element from book.xsd,
             which contains a full BookType object from book.xsd.
           -->
            <wsdl:output message="tns:GetBookResponse" name="GetBookResponse"/>

            <!--
            <wsdl:fault>
              Declares that this operation CAN throw a business error.
              The fault travels back to the client as a SOAP Fault envelope.

              message="tns:WsException"
                → References the WsException message defined above.
                  That message wraps cmn:WsFault from common.xsd.

              name="WsException"
                → The internal identifier for this fault within the operation.
                  CRITICAL: this name MUST match the fault name used in the
                  <wsdl:binding> section below. If they differ, JAX-WS will
                  not correctly serialize/deserialize the fault.
            -->
            <wsdl:fault  message="tns:WsException"     name="WsException"/>

        </wsdl:operation>
    </wsdl:portType>


    <!--
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    SECTION 4: <wsdl:binding>
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    The binding takes the ABSTRACT portType and specifies HOW it is transported.
    While portType says WHAT operations exist, binding says HOW they travel
    over the network.

    name="GetBookBinding"
      → The name of this binding. Referenced by <wsdl:service> below.

    type="tns:GetBookPortType"
      → Links this binding to our portType. This binding IS the concrete
        SOAP/HTTP implementation of the abstract GetBookPortType interface.

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    <soap:binding>
    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      Marks this as a SOAP binding (as opposed to HTTP GET, MIME, etc.)

      style="document"
        → Document/literal style: the entire body is a single XML document
          validated against the XSD schema imported in <wsdl:types>.
          The alternative is style="rpc" which is older and not recommended
          for new services.

      transport="http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/http"
        → The transport protocol is HTTP.
          This is ALWAYS this exact fixed string for SOAP over HTTP.
          It is a namespace identifier — NOT a URL to visit.

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    <wsdl:operation> inside <wsdl:binding>
    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      Every operation declared in portType MUST be listed here with its SOAP
      details. The operation names must match exactly.

      <soap:operation soapAction="GetBook"/>
        → Sets the value of the SOAPAction HTTP header sent with every request.
          This is the HTTP header that identifies which operation is being called
          WITHOUT needing to parse the XML body.
          Benefits:
            1. Firewalls and proxies can route/filter by SOAPAction
            2. Logging and monitoring can identify operations from headers alone
            3. Some legacy SOAP routers require a non-empty SOAPAction
          We can still use soapAction="" (empty string).
          Here we use the operation name — the enterprise standard practice.

      <wsdl:input> / <wsdl:output>:
        name="GetBookRequest" / name="GetBookResponse"
          → Must match the names declared in portType's operation above.

        <soap:body use="literal"/>
          → "literal" means the XML body content IS the XSD-defined element,
            taken literally — no extra SOAP encoding transformations.
            The alternative "encoded" (SOAP encoding) is deprecated and
            must never be used in modern document/literal services.

      <wsdl:fault>:
        name="WsException"
          → MUST match the fault name in portType. If mismatched, the JAX-WS
            runtime cannot pair up the fault declaration with the binding.
        <soap:fault name="WsException" use="literal"/>
          → Tells the SOAP processor to serialize the fault data literally
            from the WsFault XSD element. Same "literal" rule as the body.
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    -->
    <wsdl:binding name="GetBookBinding" type="tns:GetBookPortType">
        <soap:binding
                style="document"
                transport="http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/http"/>

        <wsdl:operation name="GetBook">
            <soap:operation soapAction="GetBook"/>

            <wsdl:input  name="GetBookRequest">
                <soap:body use="literal"/>
            </wsdl:input>

            <wsdl:output name="GetBookResponse">
                <soap:body use="literal"/>
            </wsdl:output>

            <wsdl:fault name="WsException">
                <soap:fault name="WsException" use="literal"/>
            </wsdl:fault>
        </wsdl:operation>
    </wsdl:binding>


    <!--
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    SECTION 5: <wsdl:service>
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    The service element is the CONCRETE NETWORK LOCATION of the service.
    This is the only section that typically changes between environments —
    dev, test, staging, and production all have different URLs but the same
    types, messages, portType, and binding.

    name="GetBookService"
      → Matches serviceName="GetBookService" in @WebService on GetBookPortTypeImpl.java
      → This is also the name of the generated Java factory class that wsimport
        creates for the client: GetBookService.java
      → The client instantiates it as: new GetBookService(new URL(wsdlUrl))

    <wsdl:port>
      A service can technically have multiple ports (same service exposed via
      different protocols or at different addresses). We have one port.

      name="GetBookPort"
        → Matches portName="GetBookPort" in @WebService on GetBookPortTypeImpl.java
        → Drives the name of the factory method wsimport generates:
            GetBookService.getGetBookPort()  ← returns the proxy object

      binding="tns:GetBookBinding"
        → References the <wsdl:binding> from Section 4.
          This tells the client: "use GetBookBinding rules to encode/decode
          messages when talking to this port."

      <soap:address location="http://book-server:8080/getbook"/> will be used.
        → THE ACTUAL URL WHERE THE SERVICE ACCEPTS SOAP REQUESTS.
        → "book-server" is the Docker container hostname — inside Docker's
          network, containers resolve each other by their service name as
          defined in docker-compose.yml.
        → From your HOST machine: http://localhost:8080/getbook
          (port 8080 is mapped to the host in docker-compose.yml)
        → The client can OVERRIDE this URL at runtime using BindingProvider:
            ((BindingProvider) port).getRequestContext()
              .put(BindingProvider.ENDPOINT_ADDRESS_PROPERTY, "http://...");
          This is standard practice — the WSDL address is the default,
          but configuration can override it per environment.
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    -->
    <wsdl:service name="GetBookService">
        <wsdl:port binding="tns:GetBookBinding" name="GetBookPort">
            <soap:address location="REPLACE_WITH_ACTUAL_URL"/>
        </wsdl:port>
    </wsdl:service>

</wsdl:definitions>
